  "strategy": "Approach and rationale:\n\nThe central idea is to minimize the maximum linear auto-convolution of a sequence while keeping the sum fixed. This requires broad optimization over sequences of different lengths, and our goal is to find sequences with lower objectives than the previous 1.502.\n\nWe use an annealing schedule that decreases the time budget for longer sequences, optimizing the linear program (LP) problem in decreasing time. We start with a sequence length around 768 (commonly yielding good results) and gradually decrease to shorter lengths to balance weaker attacksurface with execution time.\n\nMathematical specification:\n\nWe seek a sequence `a` of length `n` (variable) with non-negative elements and a sum at least 0.01 such that:\n\n`minimize 2 * n * max_{0<=k<=2n-2} (a * convolution)_k / (sum(a))^2`\n\nBut note: due to scaling invariance, we can fix the sum `sum(a) = 1` (then the objective becomes `2 * n * max_conv`).\n\nThe constraints are implicit in the definition of the auto-convolution and the non-negativity.\n\nImplementation procedure:\n\n1. Start with a candidate sequence for length 768, computed from a Gaussian function and simplex projection.\n2. Allocate:\n   - 400 seconds for length ~768\n   - 200 seconds for each subsequent length (512, 384, ...) but decreasing by 150 seconds each time.\n3. Use an LP solver to find the direction for improvement.\n4. Use coordinate descent as the target optimization.\n5. Use subgradient refinement to polish the result.\n6. Evaluate candidates frequently but ensure exact validation only for the saved best candidate.\n7. Maintain a global best candidate throughout the search.\n8. Try multiple sequence lengths up to 1000 to increase the number of parameters.\n9. Use exponential weights and summation of dot products for the auto-convolution.\n10. When time is tight, revert to subgradient and Very Simple methods.\n\nSearch choices and useful variations:\n\n- Try lengths in descending order: 1024, 768, 512, 256, 128, 64\n- Allocate time budget in a decreasing manner: 600 seconds initially, but dynamically reducing based on length.\n- Use simplex projection to enforce non-negativity and sum=1.\n- Use a try-sequence evaluation every 10 steps.\n\nBudget and robustness:\n\n- Time for one optimization: Will be dynamic, but start with long sequences and reduce.\n- This implementation uses Monte Carlo methods for short sequences but ensures worst-case.\n- Fallback to a uniform distribution of length 384 if no better candidate is found.\n\nImplementation:\n\nWe'll generate the following plan for the coder agent. Note that the `evaluate_sequence` function already handles bounds and zeroing.\n\n```python\n\ndef propose_candidate(\n\n    seed: int = 42,\n\n    time_limit: int = 1800,\n\n    **kwargs\n\n) -> (np.ndarray, float):\n\n    # --- Initialization ---\n\n    np.random.default_rng(seed)\n\n    current_best_score = float('inf')\n\n    current_best_sequence = None\n\n    # Compute the allocation for each length: decreasing time for longer lengths\n\n    lengths = [1024, 768, 512, 384, 256, 128, 64]\n\n    # Map each length to a time budget allocation (in descending order)\n\n    time_allocations = [600, 300, 150, 75, 60, 30, 15]  # seconds, also in descending order\n\n    # For each length (except the last one) we'll also try diagonal steps.\n\n    current_time_remaining = time_limit\n\n    # For each length, allocate time and optimize if possible.\n\n    for i, n in enumerate(lengths):\n\n        if current_time_remaining < 5: # Infeasible to do any full optimization\n\n            break\n\n        budget = time_allocations[i]\n\n        # Initialize candidate sequence\n\n        # We'll try two seeds for each length to increase the number of parameters.\n\n        if n > 768:  # Use exponential distribution for large arrays\n\n            # But note: n cannot be too large because fast_convolve may not scale.\n\n            # For n>1024, break to avoid too slow evaluations.\n\n            if n > 1024:\n\n                n = 1024\n\n                // Limit by time.\n\n            # Initialize with exponential decay\n\n            tau = n / 10.0  # Scale according to length\n\n            a = np.exp(- (np.arange(n) + np.random.uniform(-5.0, 5.0)) / tau)\n\n            a = np.maximum(a, 0)\n\n        else:  # Use Gaussian for shorter arrays\n\n            a = np.exp(-0.5 * (np.arange(n) - n/2)**2 / variance)\n\n            variance = n / 4.0\n\n        # Normalize a to sum 1\n\n        s = np.sum(a)\n\n        if s < 1e-10:\n\n            # Back up to try if empty, use uniform vector\n\n            a = np.ones(n) / n\n\n        else:\n\n            a = a / s\n\n        # Evaluate the sequence if it's feasible and finite\n\n        score = evaluate_sequence(a.tolist())\n\n        if not np.isfinite(score):\n\n            # Not a valid sequence, try smaller ones?\n\n            best_candidate = np.ones(384) / 384\n\n            best_score = evaluate_sequence(best_candidate.tolist())\n\n            continue\n\n        if score < current_best_score:\n\n            current_best_score = score\n\n            current_best_sequence = a.copy()\n\n        # Compute the auto-convolution (save the simplest method for speed)\n\n        conv = fast_convolve(a)\n\n        if np.isnan(score) or score >= current_best_score - 0.001:  // Tolerance for convergence\n\n            # Move to the next length\n\n            continue\n\n        # If we have time, do an LP optimization to find better sequences\n\n        if current_time_remaining >= 10:\n\n            a_opt = lp_fw_optimize(a, conv, budget, seed=seed+i)\n\n            if a_opt is None:\n\n                continue\n\n            score_opt = evaluate_sequence(a_opt.tolist())\n\n            if score_opt < current_best_score:\n\n                current_best_sequence = a_opt\n\n                current_best_score = score_opt\n\n        elif current_time_remaining > 0:\n\n            # Use coordinate descent for shorter times\n\n            a_opt = coordinate_descent(a, int(current_time_remaining))\n\n            score_opt = evaluate_sequence(a_opt.tolist())\n\n            if score_opt < current_best_score:\n\n                current_best_sequence = a_opt\n\n                current_best_score = score_opt\n\n        else:\n\n            # Fallback to subgradient method\n\n            a_opt = sg_polish(a, int(current_time_remaining))\n\n            score_opt = evaluate_sequence(a_opt.tolist())\n\n            if score_opt < current_best_score:\n\n                current_best_sequence = a_opt\n\n                current_best_score = score_opt\n\n    return [{'values': current_best_sequence.tolist(), 'obj': current_best_score}], current_best_score\n\n\ndef fast_convolve(a: np.ndarray) -> np.ndarray: \n    # Linear auto-convolution \n\n    n = a.shape[0]\n    # Extend the array for borders? No, use full convolution\n\n    # They said linear auto-convolution, so we do:\n\n    size = 2*n - 1\n    fft_size = 1 << (size-1).bit_length()  // power of 2 >= size\n\n    A = np.fft.rfft(a, fft_size)\n    B = np.conj(A)  // But usually for convolution, just A*A if real-friendly\n\n    if np.isreal(a).all():\n        B = np.fft.rfft(a, fft_size)  // This is conjugate if a is real, but these are both a.\n\n    conv_fft = A * A  // This is the cross-correlation, but with full convolution.\n\n    conv = np.fft.irfft(conv_fft, fft_size)[:size]  // Actually, the full convolution has length 2*n-1\n\n    return conv  // But the previous program did not clip or anything, so we must be careful\n\n\ndef lp_fw_optimize(a: np.ndarray, conv: np.ndarray, time_budget: int, seed: int) -> np.ndarray:\n\n    # Given a sequence 'a' (normal incumbent) and the convolution 'conv', we want to upgrade\n\n    # but note: the incum a is passed\n\n    # Let's map the method to the given for the previous program.\n\n    # This will follow the same high-level code as provided.\n\n    # However, we must 